<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The TSX reached its June 5 all-time high of 35,217 before the U.S. May payroll report reset rate expectations and drove the index 2.28% lower on June 6. The green shading marks the record session; the red segment marks the reversal.</p>

<p>The practitioner implication is narrow and specific. The advisor''s job on Monday is not to provide a market outlook. The market outlook is uncertain and unknowable. The job is to reframe the reference point before a client establishes Thursday''s 35,217 as the permanent psychological anchor. That conversation takes approximately two minutes if initiated proactively, and it is the difference between a client who holds through the next session and one who exits with a tax event and a realized loss on a position that was still well ahead of its cost base.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who checked their portfolios Thursday feel robbed. The sequence, record high one day, sharp decline the next, activates a loss framing even for clients whose year-to-date position is strongly positive. Energy and materials holders who drove outperformance through the Hormuz period are particularly vulnerable to a "I should have sold at the top" narrative that is emotionally compelling and analytically wrong. Clients who saw the TSX record on Thursday and did nothing will experience Friday''s decline as the consequence of inaction, not as a market event that was not predictable.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I wanted to reach you before the week gets going. You probably saw the TSX fell about 2.3% Friday after the U.S. jobs report came in much stronger than expected. That pushed bond yields up and took the whole North American market down.

Here is what I want you to hold onto: the TSX hit an all-time high Thursday. That record is now the reference point your brain is using to measure Friday''s decline, which makes it feel bigger than it is. The index is still up more than 10% for the year. Your energy positions are still substantially above where they were in February before the Hormuz situation started.

Nothing about the investment thesis has changed. The jobs number is actually good news for the economy, even though it moved the bond market in a way that hit equities short-term. The Bank of Canada is making a rate decision Wednesday. We should talk before then if you want to walk through what to expect. Nothing I am seeing right now changes our plan.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients who were told about or noticed Thursday''s record high and then checked Friday''s close. The sequence comparison is the emotional driver. Clients who only check monthly will not be affected.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with significant energy and materials positions who have large unrealized gains. They are in the best portfolio position but face the strongest disposition-effect pull toward locking in the gain before it "disappears."</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients who have been underweight equities due to volatility anxiety and who see Friday''s decline as confirmation of their caution. This is an opportunity to reframe their defensive positioning as having cost them the record-high gain, not as having protected them from the decline.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients who hold positions with large unrealized gains from the Hormuz rally. These are the clients most likely to call about taking profits after seeing Thursday to Friday.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review which clients received Thursday''s record-high notification from their brokerage or investment app. Proactive outreach before they call is the intervention that works.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a brief summary of the year-to-date TSX performance (up 10%+) to use as the alternative reference point in the Monday conversation.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note the Bank of Canada decision Wednesday June 10. Any clients asking about repositioning should be told the conversation happens after the rate decision, not before it.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document all client conversations about the Friday decline. If a client insists on selling, the documentation of the advice given is essential.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> TSX update and what I want you to know before the week starts<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
I wanted to send a quick note after Friday''s session before the week gets started. The TSX fell about 2.3% on Friday following a stronger-than-expected U.S. jobs report that pushed bond yields higher and brought North American equity markets down broadly.<br><br>
The context I want you to keep in mind: the TSX set an all-time high on Thursday, and the index is still up more than 10% for the year. Friday''s decline was a meaningful single-session move, but it did not change the underlying picture for Canadian portfolios or for your specific positions.<br><br>
The Bank of Canada makes a rate decision this Wednesday. I will follow up after that announcement with any implications for our plan. In the meantime, nothing about our strategy has changed. If you have questions or want to talk through anything specific, I am available.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors who experienced the Thursday-Friday sequence:</strong> Self-directed investors are most likely to have checked Thursday''s close, seen the record, and then checked Friday''s close with no advisor to provide context. The gap is the advisory opportunity.</p>
<p><strong>Recently retired or near-retirement clients elsewhere:</strong> This population has a lower loss tolerance and is most affected by reference-point shifts. They are also the most likely to make irreversible portfolio decisions based on a two-day market sequence.</p>
<p><strong>Investors whose energy holdings have produced significant unrealized gains since February:</strong> These clients have money in motion psychologically and are actively reconsidering whether to hold. Many lack the framework to distinguish between "locking in a gain" and "executing a disposition-effect trade."</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am calling because the TSX hit an all-time high Thursday and then fell 2.3% Friday, and I know from experience that sequence is the one that prompts people to make changes they often regret. I wanted to talk before you did anything.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The record-high-to-reversal pattern is one of the most reliable triggers for poor retail investment decisions. Research by Barber and Odean at the University of California has documented that investors who sell after sharp declines following highs systematically underperform investors who hold, because they exit before the recovery and re-enter at higher prices. A self-directed investor has no one to call when Thursday becomes Friday. An advisor with a behavioural finance framework changes the conversation before it becomes a transaction.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How often do you check your portfolio, and did you see Thursday''s record close?</p>
<p>When you saw Friday''s decline, what was the first thing you thought about doing?</p>
<p>Are there specific positions you have been watching that you were considering selling?</p>
<p>How did you handle the sharp market moves earlier this spring when the Hormuz situation escalated?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The TSX hit a record Thursday and fell 2.3% Friday. Here is what that means.<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
The TSX set an all-time high on Thursday, June 5. On Friday it fell 2.3% after a stronger-than-expected U.S. jobs report changed the outlook for interest rates.<br><br>
That two-day sequence is one of the most common triggers for poor investment decisions. When a portfolio drops from a record, the decline feels larger than it is because the new high became the reference point. Research in behavioural finance has documented this pattern for decades, and it consistently leads self-directed investors to sell at the wrong time.<br><br>
If you are managing your own investments and you found yourself thinking about making changes over the weekend, that reaction is worth a conversation. I work with clients specifically on navigating these kinds of moments. I would be glad to talk through your current situation with no commitment required.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
