  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who built meaningful energy positions through the Hormuz rally are sitting on large unrealized gains in non-registered accounts. With the TSX near record levels, the instinct to shelter those gains inside a TFSA is strong and financially rational. Clients may feel proactive and financially savvy for wanting to act. The advisory challenge is redirecting that instinct toward the correct execution method without deflating their initiative or alarming them about a transaction they may have already made.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I want to talk through the TFSA contribution you were thinking about. The goal, getting those energy gains sheltered for future growth, is exactly right. The method matters though, and there is a specific rule that catches a lot of clients by surprise.

If you transfer securities directly into the TFSA without selling them first, the CRA treats it as if you sold them at that moment. Any gain you have built up since you bought them is taxable in this year''s return, even though you never actually received any cash. On a position that is up 30 or 40 percent since February, that can be a meaningful tax bill you were not expecting.

The fix is straightforward: we sell the position in the non-registered account first, which crystallizes the gain in a controlled way, and then contribute the cash to the TFSA. The tax consequence is the same, but it is planned and we know the number in advance. Then we can repurchase the position inside the TFSA and all future growth is sheltered.

If any of your positions are actually at a loss right now, the in-kind transfer is even more important to avoid. A loss on a direct transfer into the TFSA is permanently denied. It disappears entirely.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with appreciated energy, materials, or commodity positions in non-registered accounts who are considering TFSA contributions. Any client with available TFSA room and positions with unrealized gains from the Hormuz rally is in the planning window now.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients who have already made in-kind transfers in 2026. The gain is triggered. The planning task shifts to ensuring it is reported correctly on the 2026 return and a T5008 or Schedule 3 entry is prepared.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with energy positions at a loss relative to their Hormuz-era purchase price. The advisor''s intervention preserves a loss that would be permanently destroyed by an in-kind transfer.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review non-registered account holdings for all clients with meaningful energy or commodity exposure. Identify positions carrying unrealized gains relative to pre-Hormuz purchase prices from before February 28.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag any client with available 2026 TFSA room who has mentioned wanting to contribute appreciated securities. Proactive outreach before the transaction is the intervention that prevents the error.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For clients who have already made in-kind transfers in 2026, confirm the fair market value at the date of transfer and whether a T5008 was issued. Schedule 3 reporting is required regardless of slip issuance.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For clients with energy positions at a loss, document the superficial loss rule analysis before recommending a sell-and-repurchase strategy. The 30-day window and the choice of replacement security require deliberate attention.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Before you contribute to your TFSA: one rule worth knowing<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
I wanted to follow up on our conversation about your TFSA contribution. There is a specific CRA rule that applies when you transfer securities directly into a TFSA without selling them first, and it can produce a tax surprise that is worth avoiding.<br><br>
When you contribute securities in-kind to a TFSA, the CRA treats the transfer as a deemed disposition at fair market value on the date of the contribution. Any unrealized gain on that position is taxable in the year of transfer, even though no cash changed hands. On positions that have appreciated significantly through recent market events, that can mean a meaningful tax bill you were not expecting.<br><br>
The planning solution is straightforward: sell the securities in your non-registered account first, then contribute the cash proceeds to your TFSA. I would like to walk through your specific holdings before any contributions are made this year. I will be in touch this week to set a time.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors with self-directed non-registered accounts:</strong> These clients execute their own transactions and are most likely to make an in-kind transfer without advisor guidance. They are also least likely to understand the deemed disposition rule until they receive a surprise at tax filing time.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners and incorporated professionals:</strong> This population often holds significant non-registered portfolios alongside corporate accounts. After the Hormuz-driven commodity rally, they are likely reviewing TFSA room utilization as part of year-end planning.</p>
<p><strong>Near-retirement clients with unused TFSA room:</strong> Large non-registered portfolios, significant unused room, and strong instinct to shelter gains before retirement income changes. The in-kind trap is particularly costly for those with large accrued positions on energy names.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am calling because with the TSX near record levels and energy positions showing strong gains, a lot of clients are thinking about TFSA contributions right now. There is a specific CRA rule that applies when you transfer securities directly rather than contributing cash, and I want to make sure you have that information before you act.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The CRA''s deemed disposition rule on in-kind TFSA contributions is one of the most reliably misunderstood tax rules in Canadian financial planning. It catches clients who are actively trying to do the right thing. An advisor who intercepts this error before it happens provides measurable, documentable tax value. For a client with $50,000 in appreciated energy positions, the advisor''s intervention is potentially worth several thousand dollars in avoided tax on a transaction they were about to execute without guidance.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Do you have available TFSA contribution room for 2026, and have you been thinking about using it before year-end?</p>
<p>Are there positions in your non-registered account that have appreciated significantly over the past several months?</p>
<p>Have you made any in-kind transfers to your TFSA earlier this year that we should review for tax reporting purposes?</p>
<p>How does your current advisor handle the tax planning conversation around TFSA contributions and non-registered portfolio management?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> A TFSA rule worth knowing before you contribute this year<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
With the TSX near all-time highs and energy positions showing significant gains from earlier this year, many investors are thinking about TFSA contributions right now. There is a CRA rule that applies specifically to in-kind contributions that produces unexpected tax bills for investors who are not aware of it.<br><br>
When you transfer appreciated securities directly into a TFSA without selling them first, the CRA treats the transfer as a deemed sale at current market value. The gain is taxable in the year of transfer even though no cash changed hands. On positions that have gained 30 to 50 percent over the past several months, that can be a significant and entirely avoidable surprise.<br><br>
The planning fix is simple, but you need to know about it before you execute the transaction. I would be glad to walk through your situation with no commitment required.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
