<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The GoC 5-year yield bottomed in late 2025 during the BoC easing cycle and reversed sharply after the February 28 Hormuz disruption as inflation expectations repriced. The spread between the 5-year yield and the BoC overnight rate defines the premium borrowers pay on fixed mortgage products.</p>

<h2>What Wednesday''s Statement Must Accomplish</h2>

<p>Macklem''s task Wednesday is to hold without appearing passive. The Governing Council needs to acknowledge the strong U.S. jobs print and the resulting upward pressure on GoC yields without implying that a Fed hike would trigger a corresponding BoC move. Canada and the U.S. are running different economic trajectories, and the Bank has said clearly since April that it is managing a domestic economy absorbing a supply shock, not a demand-driven inflation problem.</p>

<p>The forward guidance language that matters most is anything touching the inflation path. If the Bank signals confidence that the April 2.8% reading represents the peak, consistent with its MPR projection, that anchors expectations for a gradual decline toward 2% through 2027 and keeps rate cut optionality alive for later in 2026. If the statement expresses concern about secondary effects from oil prices or CAD weakness, the market will read that as a hold that extends further than previously assumed.</p>

<p>The June 10 decision has no Monetary Policy Report attached. The next MPR comes July 15. That means Wednesday''s press conference statement is the primary signal, and Macklem''s answers to questions about the Fed divergence will be the most closely watched exchange.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with variable-rate mortgages or upcoming fixed-rate renewals are anxious. Friday''s strong U.S. jobs report, combined with the TSX''s sharp decline, reads as a confusing signal: good economic news produced a bad market day, and the rate environment moved in the wrong direction. Clients approaching renewal in 2026 or 2027 are recalculating payment increases and looking for any signal about whether the Bank of Canada will ease further before their renewal date. The Wednesday decision is on their radar even if they cannot fully articulate why.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The Bank of Canada makes its rate decision Wednesday. I want you to know what to expect before the announcement lands.

The most likely outcome is another hold at 2.25%. The Bank held in April, March, and January. Canada''s inflation came in at 2.8% in April, which sounds high but is almost entirely energy-related. Strip out gasoline and it was 2.0%. The Bank''s own forecast had inflation peaking right about there before declining through this year.

What changed Friday is that the U.S. jobs report was very strong, much stronger than expected, and that pushed U.S. bond yields higher. Canadian yields followed. That matters for fixed mortgage rates, which are priced off the 5-year Government of Canada bond, not the Bank of Canada rate directly.

For your renewal: the decision Wednesday does not change your fixed rate environment in the short term, because that is driven by the bond market, not the overnight rate. What you want to watch is whether the Bank signals confidence that inflation is on track down. If they do, that is supportive of rates eventually declining. If they sound more cautious, the bond market may push yields higher before they come down.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with five-year fixed mortgages originated in 2021 facing renewal in 2026. They are renewing from rates near 2% into an environment where the GoC-5y is above 3%. The Wednesday statement affects the forward rate path relevant to their renewal decision.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Variable-rate mortgage holders. The BoC hold means their rate does not move Wednesday. The risk is whether a prolonged hold, followed by a Fed hike cycle, keeps Canadian variable rates elevated longer than expected.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients in GICs or high-interest savings products who benefit from a higher-for-longer rate environment if the BoC signals caution about easing.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients with mortgage renewals falling in the next six to eighteen months. Contact them before the Wednesday decision to set context for the announcement.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a brief summary of the BoC rate path since January 2025: seven cuts totalling 275 basis points, then a hold at 2.25% since January 2026. This context reframes the hold as consolidation after a major easing cycle, not a pause before tightening.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review fixed income holdings for clients with significant GoC or corporate bond exposure. Rising 5-year yields create mark-to-market pressure on longer-duration holdings.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document client conversations about the rate decision and mortgage renewal planning. If a client makes a renewal decision based on rate expectations, the documentation of advice given is important.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Bank of Canada decision Wednesday: what to expect<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
The Bank of Canada announces its rate decision this Wednesday, June 10. I wanted to give you a brief preview so you have context when the announcement lands.<br><br>
The most likely outcome is a hold at 2.25%, the same rate since January. Canada''s inflation rose to 2.8% in April, but that increase is almost entirely energy-related. Excluding gasoline, inflation ran at 2.0%, and the Bank''s own forecast had inflation peaking right around where it landed before declining through the rest of the year.<br><br>
Friday''s strong U.S. jobs report pushed bond yields higher, which affects fixed mortgage rates. But the Bank of Canada''s overnight rate decision Wednesday is separate from that bond market move, and a hold does not change your variable rate directly.<br><br>
I will follow up Wednesday after the announcement with any implications for your specific situation. If you have a mortgage renewal coming up, now is a good time to talk through your options. I am available this week if you want to connect.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Mortgage holders approaching renewal in 2026 or 2027:</strong> The combination of a Wednesday BoC decision, a strong U.S. jobs report, and rising bond yields creates the rate-anxiety moment that motivates prospects to want an advisor who can explain the environment.</p>
<p><strong>Recent homebuyers on variable-rate mortgages:</strong> Entered the market during the rate easing cycle expecting rates to continue declining. Friday''s repricing of Fed expectations introduces uncertainty they did not plan for.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners with commercial lending exposure:</strong> Floating-rate business loans are directly affected by the BoC overnight rate path. The Wednesday decision and the forward guidance language are directly relevant to their borrowing costs.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am calling ahead of Wednesday''s Bank of Canada decision because I know a lot of people saw the markets fall Friday after a strong U.S. jobs report and are wondering what that means for Canadian rates. I wanted to walk through what to expect before the announcement.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The gap between the Bank of Canada rate, the Government of Canada 5-year bond yield, and the actual mortgage rate a client pays is not well understood by most borrowers. An advisor who can explain how Friday''s U.S. jobs number moved Canadian bond yields, and what that means for a renewal coming up in 2026, is providing information that has direct dollar value to the client. Most mortgage holders make renewal decisions based on whatever rate their bank quotes them. A client with an advisor makes a decision based on a rate path analysis.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>When does your mortgage come up for renewal, and have you started looking at rate options?</p>
<p>Did you see the markets fall Friday after the U.S. jobs report, and did you have questions about what that meant for Canadian rates?</p>
<p>Are you currently on a fixed or variable rate, and how has the rate environment over the past two years affected your payments?</p>
<p>Have you had a conversation with your advisor or bank about the rate environment going into your renewal?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Bank of Canada decides Wednesday. Here is what you need to know.<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
The Bank of Canada announces its rate decision this Wednesday, June 10. It comes 48 hours after a strong U.S. jobs report that pushed bond yields higher and sent North American equity markets sharply lower.<br><br>
For Canadian mortgage holders, the two things are related but not the same. The Bank of Canada''s overnight rate is what drives variable mortgages. The Government of Canada 5-year bond yield is what drives fixed mortgage rates. Friday''s U.S. payroll report moved the bond market, which affects fixed rates, even though the BoC has not moved its overnight rate since January.<br><br>
If you have a renewal coming up in the next year or are trying to make sense of where rates are headed, I would be glad to walk through the current picture with you. No commitment required.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
