VALUES (
  '2026/06/08/the-deal-that-isnt-hormuz-diplomacy-and-what-the-oil-market-is-pricing',
  'geo', 'article',
  'The Deal That Isn''t: Hormuz Diplomacy and What the Oil Market Is Pricing',
  'Trump says a deal is largely negotiated. Iran says the description is incomplete and inconsistent with reality. WTI at $90 is the market''s verdict on who is right.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>On May 23, President Trump posted that an agreement to reopen the Strait of Hormuz had been "largely negotiated,"</strong><span> citing the United States, Iran, and unnamed third countries. Iran''s foreign ministry disputed the characterization within hours, calling it "incomplete and inconsistent with reality."</span></li>
<li><strong>Iran suspended negotiations entirely on June 1</strong><span> after accusing the U.S. of ceasefire violations linked to Israel''s continued military operations in Lebanon, demanding a full Israeli withdrawal before any resumption of dialogue through Pakistani intermediaries.</span></li>
<li><strong>A separate Iranian proposal, delivered via Omani and Pakistani channels,</strong><span> offered to reopen the strait and extend the ceasefire without addressing the nuclear file, postponing enrichment negotiations to a later stage. The White House received the proposal but has not publicly embraced it, as it would remove the primary leverage for nuclear concessions.</span></li>
<li><strong>WTI crude settled near $90 on Friday</strong><span> after falling roughly 3% on Chinese demand weakness data and partial deal optimism. The oil market is currently pricing a base case of continued partial disruption with no full reopening, not the optimistic scenario Trump''s May 23 post implied.</span></li>
<li><strong>For Canadian portfolios,</strong><span> the $90 WTI floor matters more than the direction of any single day''s move. Canadian energy producers remain substantially above pre-conflict levels, and the TSX Energy sub-index has outperformed the broad composite by more than 15 percentage points since February 28.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The diplomatic picture around the Strait of Hormuz entering this week is best described as a structured stalemate. The United States and Iran are not at war and not at peace. They are exchanging proposals through Pakistani and Omani intermediaries while conducting intermittent military incidents that each side characterizes as the other''s violation of the April 8 ceasefire. WTI crude at $90 is the oil market''s real-time assessment of how that stalemate resolves, and the assessment is: not soon, and not cleanly.</p>

<p>President Trump''s May 23 Truth Social post claiming an agreement had been "largely negotiated" produced an immediate market reaction, pulling oil prices lower on reopening optimism. Within hours, Iran''s foreign ministry spokesperson described the characterization as "incomplete and inconsistent with reality." The divergence between the two positions was not a matter of emphasis. Trump mentioned reopening the strait. He did not mention Iran''s nuclear program, the disposition of highly enriched uranium, or sanctions relief, all of which Iran has said are prerequisites for any comprehensive settlement. Iran''s statement made clear those omissions were not accidental.</p>

<h2>The Structural Obstacle</h2>

<p>The core impasse has been consistent since April. Iran wants to separate the strait reopening from nuclear negotiations, arguing that the two issues should be sequenced: ceasefire and maritime access first, nuclear discussions after. The White House has resisted this sequencing because agreeing to it removes the primary source of leverage over Tehran''s enrichment program. If the strait reopens before nuclear concessions are secured, the economic pressure on Iran eases, and the incentive to make nuclear concessions diminishes accordingly.</p>

<p>Iran submitted a new proposal in late May, delivered through Omani and Pakistani channels, that offered exactly this decoupled sequence: permanent ceasefire, strait reopening, sanctions relief, with nuclear talks deferred to a separate and later process. The Financial Times reported the framework would unfreeze Iranian overseas assets as part of the first-phase agreement. The White House acknowledged receipt of the proposal without endorsing it.</p>

<p>The IAEA''s position complicates any framework that defers nuclear talks. IAEA Director-General Rafael Grossi stated in April that any agreement without provisions for resumed inspections would be "an illusion of an agreement." The agency has been unable to verify the extent of damage to Iran''s nuclear infrastructure from the February-March strikes, and without inspections access, the disposition of Iran''s highly enriched uranium stockpile remains unverifiable.</p>

<h2>The June 1 Suspension and What Followed</h2>

<p>Iran''s decision on June 1 to suspend negotiations through Pakistani intermediaries, citing Israeli military operations in Lebanon as ceasefire violations, introduced a new variable. Iran''s state-affiliated Tasnim news agency reported that Tehran would move to "completely" close the strait in retaliation, a threat that produced a brief oil price spike before partially reversing. Iran subsequently conducted a drone activity incident near U.S. naval assets and the U.S. struck Iranian radar sites, both characterized by their respective sides as defensive responses.</p>

<p>The Polymarket prediction market tracking a permanent US-Iran peace deal by various dates showed the June 30 probability declining sharply after the June 1 suspension announcement before partially recovering as the rhetoric cooled. The current market pricing implies roughly a 30% probability of a meaningful Hormuz reopening framework before September, with the base case being a continuation of the partial disruption and fragile ceasefire through the summer.</p>

<h2>What $90 WTI Means for Canadian Portfolios</h2>

<p>The Bank of Canada''s April Monetary Policy Report assumed Brent crude averaging $90 in Q2 and declining gradually toward $75 by mid-2027. That baseline was constructed before the May 23 Trump post and the subsequent June 1 suspension produced the current ambiguity. With Brent tracking approximately $3 to $4 above WTI, the current oil price environment is consistent with the BoC''s Q2 baseline, which means Wednesday''s rate decision is not being forced by an oil price overshoot. The scenario that changes the BoC''s calculus is a renewed escalation pushing Brent back toward $110 or higher, not the current level.</p>

<p>For Canadian energy producers, the $90 WTI floor created by the Hormuz disruption represents a sustained structural support that did not exist before February 28. Suncor, Canadian Natural Resources, Cenovus, and Imperial Oil have all recalibrated their production economics around an oil price environment materially higher than the mid-$60s at which they entered the year. The TSX Energy sub-index has outperformed the broad TSX composite by more than 15 percentage points since the conflict began, and that outperformance is not threatened by a single day''s demand-driven oil price decline.</p>

<p>The geopolitical risk premium embedded in current oil prices, estimated by Goldman Sachs at approximately $12 to $15 per barrel above a no-conflict baseline, is what compresses as diplomacy advances and expands as it stalls. The current trajectory, the June 1 suspension followed by resumed back-channel contact, suggests the premium is stable rather than directionally moving. That is the portfolio relevant signal: neither the sharp expansion that would threaten the BoC''s baseline nor the sharp compression that would reprice Canadian energy names lower.</p>

<h2>The Chain to Watch This Week</h2>

<p>Three developments in the next seven days have direct Canadian portfolio implications. The Bank of Canada decision Wednesday sets the domestic rate context. Any public statement from the White House or Iranian foreign ministry on the status of negotiations between now and Wednesday''s BoC press conference creates an oil price move that Macklem will be asked to address. And OPEC+''s approved July production increase of 188,000 barrels per day, agreed despite the Hormuz uncertainty, takes effect next month and represents a modest supply-side offset to the disruption that has not yet reached refiners.</p>

<p>WTI''s trajectory from its April peak of approximately $117 toward the current $90 represents a 23% decline from the crisis high, but the price remains 38% above its pre-conflict level of roughly $65. WTI''s path from January 2026 through Friday''s close traces the conflict-driven surge, the partial ceasefire retracement, and the demand-shock decline that followed Friday''s NFP print.</p>

