<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">WTI peaked at approximately $117 per barrel in early April before declining 23% as the partial ceasefire took effect and demand concerns emerged. The $90 current level remains 38% above the pre-conflict price and is consistent with the Bank of Canada''s Q2 baseline assumption.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who followed the May 23 Trump post about a "largely negotiated" deal may have expected oil prices to fall sharply and energy positions to give back their gains. When WTI stayed near $90 and the deal failed to materialize, the reaction is a mix of continued anxiety and confusion about what is actually happening. Clients with energy holdings are watching those positions with a mix of relief that the Hormuz floor is holding and uncertainty about whether a sudden deal announcement could reprice their positions lower. Clients without energy exposure feel they missed a structural trade and are now wondering whether to add exposure at $90.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The Hormuz situation is at an impasse that the oil market has already priced. The United States and Iran are exchanging proposals through intermediaries, Trump says a deal is largely done, Iran says that description is inaccurate, and neither side has moved off their core positions on the nuclear question.

What this means practically is that the geopolitical risk premium in oil, the extra roughly $12 to $15 per barrel above where oil would trade without the conflict, is stable. It is not expanding because there is no new escalation. It is not compressing because there is no agreement. The $90 WTI level reflects that equilibrium.

For your energy positions: they are up substantially from February and they are being supported by a floor that did not exist before the conflict. The risk to those positions is a genuine, verified deal that reopens the strait, which would remove that floor. That risk is real but the evidence from Friday''s diplomacy is that it is not imminent. The scenario that hurts the portfolio is not a demand-data point, it is a framework agreement with IAEA verification provisions attached. We are not there.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated Canadian energy positions who are anxious about a sudden deal announcement compressing their gains. These clients need a clear explanation of what a genuine deal would require versus what the current diplomatic noise represents.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with balanced portfolios who have benefited from the energy weighting in the TSX but are now seeing technology and rate-sensitive positions under pressure from the NFP-driven yield move.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients in cash or underweight equities who have been waiting for a geopolitical resolution before adding risk. The $90 floor with a stable risk premium is actually a more defensible entry point than $117 was at the April peak.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For clients anxious about a deal announcement, prepare a clear summary of the three conditions that would constitute a genuine Hormuz reopening: ceasefire formalized, IAEA inspections resumed, nuclear enrichment framework agreed. None of those three conditions are currently in place.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review energy weighting in client portfolios relative to their target allocation. Positions that have grown to outsized weights due to the Hormuz rally may warrant a rebalancing conversation regardless of the geopolitical outcome.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note the OPEC+ July production increase of 188,000 barrels per day. This adds modest supply-side pressure to oil prices independent of the Hormuz situation and is worth including in any conversation about the oil price outlook.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Track the Bank of Canada Wednesday press conference specifically for Macklem''s language about the oil price baseline. Any deviation from the April MPR''s $90 Q2 assumption is a signal about the BoC''s internal read of the geopolitical risk premium.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Hormuz update and what it means for your energy positions<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
I wanted to give you a brief update on the Hormuz diplomatic situation, since it has a direct bearing on your energy positions.<br><br>
The short version: the U.S. and Iran are exchanging proposals but remain at an impasse on the core issue, which is the nuclear file. President Trump''s May 23 post about a deal being "largely negotiated" was disputed by Iran within hours, and Iran suspended negotiations through intermediaries on June 1. Talks have quietly resumed but no framework has been agreed.<br><br>
WTI crude is currently near $90 per barrel. That level reflects a stable geopolitical risk premium: no new escalation but no genuine resolution either. Your energy positions are supported by that floor, which did not exist before February 28. The scenario that would compress those positions significantly is a formal, verified deal with IAEA inspection provisions. The evidence from the current negotiations is that we are not close to that outcome.<br><br>
I will keep you updated as the situation develops. If you want to talk through the implications for your specific holdings, I am available this week.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Self-directed investors with energy exposure who are unsure whether to hold:</strong> The Trump "largely negotiated" post created confusion about whether to take profits before a deal compresses prices. These clients need a framework for distinguishing between diplomatic noise and genuine deal risk.</p>
<p><strong>Investors who sold energy on the deal optimism and missed the continued $90 floor:</strong> A significant population made the rational-seeming decision to reduce energy exposure on the May 23 post and watched the deal fail to materialize. They are now reconsidering their approach.</p>
<p><strong>High-net-worth clients with large non-registered portfolios and significant energy gains:</strong> The combination of geopolitical uncertainty, a potential deal announcement at any time, and large unrealized gains creates a planning conversation that spans both the geopolitical and tax desks.</span></p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am calling because the Hormuz situation is at a specific kind of impasse that I think is worth understanding before making any decisions about energy positions, and I wanted to walk through where things actually stand versus what the headlines have been saying.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The gap between what Trump''s Truth Social post said and what the Iranian foreign ministry said in response within hours is the kind of signal that most investors do not have the context to interpret correctly. An advisor with a geopolitical framework can distinguish between a genuine deal signal and a negotiating position, and can explain what the oil market''s $90 price level is actually communicating about the probability of each outcome. That framework is not available from watching market headlines.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Did you see the Trump post about a Hormuz deal being largely negotiated, and how did you interpret it for your portfolio?</p>
<p>Do you have energy positions, and have you been thinking about whether to hold them through the current diplomatic uncertainty?</p>
<p>How has your current advisor been communicating about the geopolitical situation and its effect on your holdings?</p>
<p>If a Hormuz reopening deal were announced tomorrow, do you have a plan for how you would respond?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Hormuz deal that isn''t: what the oil market is actually telling you<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
Two weeks ago, President Trump posted that a Hormuz agreement had been "largely negotiated." Iran''s foreign ministry called that description "incomplete and inconsistent with reality" within hours. WTI crude, which briefly dipped on the news, is back near $90 per barrel.<br><br>
The oil market is telling you something specific: it does not believe a genuine, verified deal is imminent. The $90 price level reflects a stable geopolitical risk premium on top of a pre-conflict baseline near $65. That premium compresses when a real deal happens. It does not compress on a Truth Social post.<br><br>
If you have energy positions and you have been trying to figure out whether to hold through this diplomacy, I can offer a framework for thinking about the specific conditions that would constitute a genuine deal versus what we have seen so far. I would be glad to talk through your situation. No commitment required.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
