VALUES (
  '2026/06/08/record-then-reversal-the-tsx-s-friday-anatomy-and-what-it-means-monday',
  'market', 'article',
  'Record Then Reversal: The TSX''s Friday Anatomy and What It Means Monday',
  'The TSX hit 35,217 Thursday, then shed 803 points Friday on a jobs report that repriced the Fed. The sector rotation inside that decline is the real story.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The TSX Composite fell 803 points, or 2.28%, to close at 34,413 on Friday,</strong><span> one session after setting an all-time high of 35,217. The catalyst was the U.S. May non-farm payrolls report: 172,000 jobs added against an 85,000 consensus, with prior months revised up a combined 93,000.</span></li>
<li><strong>The S&P 500 fell 2.64% and the Nasdaq dropped 4.77% on Friday,</strong><span> the sharpest single-session decline since March. Technology led the selloff as the strong jobs number pushed the U.S. 10-year yield higher, compressing the valuation multiple on long-duration growth stocks.</span></li>
<li><strong>Canadian energy held better than the broad index,</strong><span> with WTI settling near $90 after a 3% decline driven by Chinese import data showing crude imports at their lowest level in ten years. Suncor and Canadian Natural Resources closed roughly flat to modestly lower, outperforming the TSX by a substantial margin.</span></li>
<li><strong>The TSX''s year-to-date performance remains above 10% despite Friday''s reversal,</strong><span> with the index still trading well above its late-May range. The record high set Thursday was not a false breakout, it was a genuine new high reached through broad sector participation before a specific macro catalyst reversed the session.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Bank of Canada rate decision Wednesday June 10 is the next scheduled market event,</strong><span> with a hold at 2.25% near-certain. The press conference language about the inflation path and the BoC''s reaction function to Fed divergence is the variable that matters for Canadian bond yields and rate-sensitive equity sectors this week.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The TSX Composite produced one of its most instructive two-session sequences of 2026 Thursday and Friday. A fresh all-time high of 35,217 on Thursday, driven by broad sector participation including healthcare, financials, and industrials, gave way to an 803-point decline the following session after the U.S. May non-farm payrolls report arrived at more than double consensus. Friday''s close at 34,413 was the largest single-session decline in weeks. The year-to-date gain of just over 10% was intact. The record high was behind it.</p>

<p>The anatomy of Friday''s move matters as much as the magnitude. The U.S. jobs report added 172,000 positions in May against an 85,000 consensus, with upward revisions to March and April adding another 93,000 to the picture. The unemployment rate held at 4.3% and average hourly earnings rose 0.3% on the month. Markets read this as a labour market that does not need Federal Reserve support, repricing the probability of a December Fed rate hike from 45% to 61% within hours. The U.S. 10-year Treasury yield climbed. Canadian government bond yields followed. The valuation multiple on technology and growth stocks, which is inversely related to the discount rate, compressed rapidly. The Nasdaq fell 4.77%.</p>

<h2>What Moved and What Held</h2>

<p>Canadian equities did not replicate the Nasdaq''s severity. The TSX''s 2.28% decline was meaningful but substantially cushioned by its energy and materials weighting. WTI crude fell approximately 3% on Friday, closing near $90, as Chinese crude import data showed imports at their lowest level in ten years, raising demand-side concerns. But $90 WTI still represents a 38% premium to pre-conflict levels, and Canadian energy producers'' economics are set around a materially higher oil price than the mid-$60s at which they entered 2026.</p>

<p>Suncor and Canadian Natural Resources closed roughly flat to modestly lower, a substantial outperformance relative to a TSX that fell more than 2%. The TSX Energy sub-index held far better than the broader composite. Technology names including Shopify experienced sharper declines in sympathy with the Nasdaq, and financials came under rate-related pressure as the yield curve steepened. Healthcare and industrials, which led Thursday''s record-setting session, gave back some of their gains but remained the more resilient segments of the non-energy index.</p>

<p>The sector rotation inside Friday''s decline confirms the interpretation that this was a rate-repricing event, not a fundamental Canadian equity concern. When the discount rate rises, long-duration assets reprice first and fastest. Canadian energy names are short-duration in the relevant sense: their cash flows are near-term, their earnings leverage to oil is immediate, and their valuation is not dependent on a low-rate terminal assumption. This is why the TSX outperformed Wall Street even on a day when the TSX sold off.</p>

<h2>The Record High in Context</h2>

<p>Thursday''s 35,217 close was a genuine new high, not a technical overshoot. The TSX reached it through broad participation: healthcare led, financials contributed, industrials added, and the Descartes Systems 5%-plus post-earnings move gave technology a positive session. That breadth matters because it means the record was not a single-sector artifact. It was a TSX that was, for one session, firing on most cylinders simultaneously.</p>

<p>The Friday reversal was externally driven. The NFP print, the resulting yield move, the Nasdaq selloff, all transmitted mechanically into Canadian equities through cross-border capital flow and risk appetite channels. The Canadian macro picture, with BoC on hold, inflation broadly in line with projections, and energy sector fundamentals intact, did not change between Thursday''s close and Friday''s open. The record high reflected the Canadian picture. The reversal reflected the U.S. one.</p>

<p>This distinction is not a rationalization. It is a description of the mechanism. Advisors who can explain the mechanism to clients have a materially different conversation Monday than those who are responding to the headline number in isolation.</p>

<h2>The Setup for This Week</h2>

<p>Three events define the week. The Bank of Canada announces Wednesday, with a hold at 2.25% fully priced. The press conference matters: any signal that the Bank is more concerned about upside inflation risk from CAD weakness and persistent energy prices than the April statement implied would push Canadian bond yields higher and add further pressure to rate-sensitive equity sectors. The more likely outcome is language that reaffirms the April MPR baseline, which is supportive of a continued hold without implying tightening.</p>

<p>US-Iran diplomacy remains the background variable. Any credible deal signal, which requires movement on the nuclear file rather than just strait access, would pull WTI materially lower and reprice the TSX Energy sub-index. The June 1 suspension of negotiations and the subsequent slow resumption of back-channel contact suggest that signal is not imminent this week, but it cannot be ruled out on any given day.</p>

<p>The OPEC+ July production increase of 188,000 barrels per day, approved despite the Hormuz uncertainty, takes effect next month. It represents a modest supply addition that will not offset the Hormuz disruption but adds to the modest downward pressure on oil that Chinese demand data introduced Friday. The $90 WTI level is not under serious technical threat from the supply side alone.</p>

<p>TSX Composite sector performance since the Hormuz disruption began February 28, plotted through Friday''s close, shows the bifurcated return profile that defines Canadian equity risk in this environment: energy and materials substantially positive, technology flat to negative, financials range-bound as rate sensitivity and margin pressure compete.</p>

<div class="hdq-chart">
