<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Energy''s 34.1% gain since February 28 reflects the direct pass-through of WTI''s Hormuz-driven surge into Canadian producer economics. Technology''s 5.8% decline from the same start date reflects rate sensitivity compounded by the Friday NFP-driven yield move.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients are processing a jarring sequence: all-time high Thursday, sharp decline Friday. Those with energy-heavy portfolios are relieved that their positions held but uncertain whether the TSX record is now in the rearview mirror for an extended period. Clients without energy exposure are looking at a portfolio that underperformed the TSX''s Hormuz trade and also participated in Friday''s decline. The Bank of Canada decision Wednesday adds another potential source of anxiety for clients with mortgage exposure. The overall emotional register is: confused, not panicked, and waiting to be told what to make of it.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The TSX hit an all-time high Thursday and fell 2.3% Friday. I want to give you the clean version of what happened.

Friday''s decline was driven entirely by a U.S. jobs report that came in much stronger than expected. That pushed U.S. bond yields higher, which compressed the valuation on technology stocks. The Nasdaq fell almost 5%. The TSX fell 2.3%, much less, because our market has significant energy weighting and energy held relatively well.

The Canadian fundamentals did not change between Thursday and Friday. Inflation is broadly where the Bank of Canada projected it. Energy sector economics are intact with oil near $90. The Bank decides Wednesday and will almost certainly hold.

What changed Friday is U.S. rate expectations, and that is a real change: the probability of a Fed rate hike by December went from 45% to over 60%. That matters for how U.S. growth stocks are priced. It matters less directly for Canadian energy producers, financials, or utilities.

The TSX is still up more than 10% for the year. Friday was a meaningful decline from a record high. It was not a structural break.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with significant technology or growth stock exposure, whether through U.S. ETFs, Shopify, or Nasdaq-linked products. These positions repriced most severely on Friday and carry the most risk if the Fed hike repricing extends through the summer.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with balanced Canadian equity portfolios. The energy weighting cushioned Friday''s decline, but rate-sensitive sectors including financials, real estate, and utilities added to the drawdown.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with energy overweight positions who have been anxious about selling at the top. Friday demonstrated that the TSX energy sector held significantly better than the broader market during a rate-driven selloff, reinforcing the case for maintaining exposure at the current $90 WTI level.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare for Monday client contact with the two-sentence summary: Thursday was a genuine all-time high driven by broad Canadian participation. Friday was a U.S. rate-repricing event that transmitted mechanically into Canadian equities. These are different things.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review any clients with Nasdaq or U.S. technology-heavy exposure. The Friday 4.77% Nasdaq decline is the top of the range, not the whole story, if the Fed repricing extends.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients who may call to reduce equity exposure after seeing Friday''s close. Have the year-to-date TSX performance number and the sector attribution ready before those calls.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note the Bank of Canada decision Wednesday. Any clients asking about equity positioning should be told the conversation happens in full after the rate decision, not before it.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What happened Thursday and Friday and what it means Monday<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
I wanted to give you a clean summary of last week''s markets before the new week starts.<br><br>
Thursday: the TSX set an all-time high of 35,217, driven by broad gains in healthcare, financials, and industrials.<br><br>
Friday: the TSX fell 803 points to 34,413, a 2.28% decline. The cause was a U.S. jobs report that came in at more than double the expected number, pushing U.S. bond yields higher and compressing valuations on technology and growth stocks. The Nasdaq fell nearly 5%. The TSX fell considerably less because of the energy sector''s relative resilience with oil near $90.<br><br>
The TSX is still up more than 10% for the year. The Bank of Canada makes its rate decision this Wednesday and is expected to hold at 2.25%. I will follow up after the announcement.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
